title: How to write a short answer for a page
description: 'How to write a short answer: write it last, keep it to forty to sixty words, name the subject and check it makes sense alone.'
publishDate: 2026-11-03
updatedDate: 2026-11-03
shortAnswer: 'To write a short answer for a page, state the point of the page in forty to sixty words, name the subject in full and leave out any reference to the page itself. Write it last, after the rest of the page is final, then read it alone to check how it sounds when a search engine quotes it.'
fullAnswer: 'A short answer is the first paragraph of a page, and it has to work when it is quoted alone. Writing one takes four steps. First, finish the page, because the point of a page is only clear once the page is done. Second, state that point in one paragraph of forty to sixty words, starting with the result and not with the background. Third, name the subject in full, so the paragraph still makes sense when a search result shows it without the title. Fourth, read the paragraph alone and strike any word that points back at the page, such as this page, below or above. A short answer is checked, not trusted. Keep a table of the checks, run it before each page ships, and run it again whenever the page changes. A page that fails a check is edited until it passes, and its updated date changes to show the edit.'
keyTakeaways:
  - 'Write the short answer last, once the rest of the page is final.'
  - 'Use forty to sixty words, start with the result and name the subject in full.'
  - 'Read the paragraph alone and remove any word that points back at the page.'
  - 'Check every short answer against a fixed list before the page ships.'
faq:
  - q: 'How long should a short answer be?'
    a: 'Between forty and sixty words. A shorter answer tends to leave out the subject or the result, and a longer one stops being quotable because the reader has to scroll to find its end.'
  - q: 'Where does the short answer go on the page?'
    a: 'At the top, directly under the heading and before any background text. Readers and search engines look there first, so a short answer placed anywhere else may be missed.'
  - q: 'Can the short answer repeat the title?'
    a: 'It can share words with the title, but it should not only repeat them. The title names the subject, and the short answer gives the result for that subject.'
