This page shows how to write a short answer, the first paragraph a reader sees. It answers the question the page exists for, so a person or a search engine can quote it without the rest. The [glossary entry for a short answer](/glossary/short-answer) gives the definition. This page gives the steps.

## How do you write a short answer?

Write it last. A page has a point only once it is finished, and a short answer written first usually describes the plan, not the result. Open the finished page, find the sentence that states the result, and build the paragraph around it.

State the result in the first sentence. Then add the one condition or number a reader needs to use the result. Name the subject in full each time, so the paragraph still makes sense when a search result shows it alone.

## What does a good short answer check for?

Run each short answer against the same list before the page ships. The [short answer checklist](/resources/short-answer-checklist) holds the full list. The table shows the checks that catch most faults.

| Check    | What a pass looks like                | What a failure looks like                                    |
| -------- | ------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Length   | Forty to sixty words.                 | A single sentence, or a paragraph that runs past the screen. |
| Subject  | The subject is named in full.         | The text says it or this, and the reader must guess.         |
| Result   | The first sentence states the result. | The first sentence gives background.                         |
| Pointers | No word points back at the page.      | The text says this page, below or above.                     |

## Why does the short answer go first?

Readers scan before they read. [Research on how people read on the web](https://www.nngroup.com/articles/how-users-read-on-the-web/) found that most visitors do not read word by word, so the opening lines decide whether a page gets used. Search engines also look for a direct answer near the top of a page, as the [guidance on helpful content from Google](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/creating-helpful-content) explains. The [plain language guide](https://digital.gov/guides/plain-language/) gives the same advice for any reader: put the main point first.

## What goes wrong most often?

The commonest fault is an answer that describes the page instead of answering the question. Another is a paragraph that is correct but too long to quote. A third is a paragraph that relies on the title for its subject. Each fault shows up when you read the paragraph alone, which is why that check comes last.

When a page changes, check the short answer again and change the updated date. A short answer that no longer matches the page is worse than none, because a search engine may still quote it.
